WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.390
Okay.

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:02.390
Next question, David.

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.500
Will the 2020 election lead to violence in
the streets?

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.499
That could be interpreted a bunch of different
ways.

00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:13.020
If your question is violence by whom, I guess
is the question right, there could be violence

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.539
in the streets if Trump wins and there could
be violence in the streets if Trump doesn't

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:21.329
win and the violence in either or both cases
would be by different people.

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:24.869
I mean, I guess let's, let's just sort of
go through it because this is so often asked

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:31.310
if Donald Trump wins, uh, will there be violence
from leftists in the streets?

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.529
I think it is very unlikely.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:37.350
I mean, okay, we cover left wing violence
to the extent that it exists.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.629
Most of the political violence in the United
States violence over politics is right wing.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:44.440
We did a story earlier this week about it.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:50.960
Will there be violence in the streets if Trump
wins from Trumpists celebrating?

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.969
Uh, I don't know.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:53.969
Uh, probably not.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.429
I don't, I don't, I don't think so.

00:00:55.429 --> 00:01:02.260
I think the most likely source of violence
electorally would be if Donald Trump loses

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:08.170
and Jens up, uh, anger among his base over
a so-called fraudulent election.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.150
I think that's the most likely scenario to
lead to violence in the streets.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.960
Um, and I hope it doesn't happen and I think
it's really important.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.899
You know, when we talk in the lead up to an
election about we want the highest voter turnout

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:21.899
possible.

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:26.369
Part of that is the more people are engaged
with the democratic process, the more difficult

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:32.270
it is to sort of fire people up along the
basis of these fraud claims.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.920
If everybody knows they voted.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.500
And, uh, there, there is sort of an easy way
to corroborate that the results that we've

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.290
seen are the results that people believe are
the correct ones.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.649
It makes it much more difficult for authoritarian
leaning folks like Donald Trump to actually

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:49.400
fire people up and, and uh, in more significant
ways.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:55.500
I think it's also important when we talk about
securing the election, um, to understand that

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:01.570
on the back end, one of the negative impact
of not securing the election can be more of

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:02.979
an opening for violence in the streets.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.590
The, the, the likes of which I'm being asked
about here.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.229
So when we talk about voter suppression, when
we talk about foreign interference, when we

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:15.730
talk about all of these things, the more secure
we all believe our elections to be, the less

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.900
likely it is that there is going to be that
sort of violence.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.430
If, and I hope it really doesn't happen, Donald
Trump loses in November of 2020 but claims

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.000
that he was robbed in some fraudulent way
and his supporters grow up, go crazy and get

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.060
out on the streets, some portion of them will
take the bait from him and we'll do it.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.620
But if the majority of the country, if a plurality
of the country understands that no, we have

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.530
a democratic process and it worked reasonably
well, uh, there is no cause for violence here.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.140
That is a great insulator from that type of
violence.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.739
I hope that there will not be violence in
the streets.

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:57.220
And if there is, I know we have the resources
to quell it responsibly and respectfully.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.269
People who are just exercising or right to
speech should not be manhandled.

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:05.470
There shouldn't be violence done against them
by law enforcement.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.710
That's extraordinarily important to remember
as well.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:15.250
Uh, but those who are violent on the streets
do need to, uh, suffer the consequences of

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.370
that because we should be against all of those
forms of violence.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.510
No question whatsoever.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:21.620
I want to hear from you about it.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.940
What do you think is the most likely scenario
to lead to violence in the streets?

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:31.409
I think again, the most likely scenario is
violence from the right over Donald Trump

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:37.069
losing and claiming that he didn't, and saying
that he needs some kind of support in staying

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:40.239
in the White House, which I do not believe
he would ultimately be able to do.

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:41.489
Let me know what you think.

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:53.489
We've got a great bonus show for you today.

